Z||000026||Voytek||06-11-2001||02:43 PM||||First of all, no new re-design on the Camaro.  GM is killing this car next model year (I believe).<BR>Fiero 56: I agree with you on the Bonneville SSEI.  Very sweet and fast car.<BR>Pontiaddict: Deimler/Chrysler (or just Chrysler) makes some pretty nice new vehicles.  Just like any other auto maker, some are nicer than others.  I DEFINITELY don't recognize ANY of them to be copies of other automobiles.  I can't say the same for Lexus (or should I call it Mercedes - half of them look like one anyways).<BR>Back on topic, though, cars that should be killed just because I'M SICK OF THEM and they're odd looking (to say the least):<BR>1. PT Cruiser - an under-powered bread delivery truck - looks OK but too many middle aged, starved-for-attention snobs drive them<BR>2. VW Bug - same reason as above (except for the bread delivery thing)<BR>3. For Excursion / Expedition - ugly and WAAAY too huge<P>I won't put the Aztek on my list, though.  It does look odd but kinda neat at the same time.  I wouldn't buy one but time will tell if it's a good seller.<BR>As for the Grand Am, it's not a bad looking car BUT, it's way too tall and overdone on the ribbed stuff.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||Slammed Fiero||06-11-2001||03:04 PM||||Daimler Chrysler is owned by Deustchland bank in germany and the kuwaitie government...Hard to believe I know , But it's TRUE. DC was told by the parent companies that if they don't start to turn a profit they will be seperated. Hence the killing of various prototype projects such as the 300C convert rear drive hemi , and other projects. Chrysler lost billions in a time when every other automanufacter made money. <P>Chrysler builds some really nice looking cars , with bad running gear. Hence the switch over to lots of mitsubishi technology. Contrary to popular belief the PT crusier(Piece of trash crusier) doe snot share a lot with the neon. IT is however listed as being one of the most unsafe vehicles on the road. I believe the cavalier / sunfire is the most unsafe safety rating wise.<P>A well prepped miata will out handle ANY FIERO , even in stock form the miata is better than most prepped fiero's. The downfall of the Miata is the price , In Canada There over 40 grand<P>Money and income have nothing to do with intelligence. I know lots of 6-7 figure income people who are dumbasses. <P>I would drop the Crapalier and sunfire , replace it with a car with technology that is current. You could retune the old quad 4 190hp engine to be a lot like the VTEC motor, they have the technology , they have affiliation with Toyota..start using your connections. GM is filled with managers of this and that , They all think GM is number #1..far far from it. <P>GM will always be behind , they will continue to grow and not much will slow them down , no matter what pile of sh** they build<P>Kill the Aztec , the only ones I ever see have dealer plates on them. There an ugly monster that GM introduced to the Hybird /  SAV (sport activity vehicle) market too late.<BR> According to automotive news the Neon is already on the chop block. They have to be the biggest piles of Junk I have ever ridden in.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.<p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||JSocha||06-11-2001||04:19 PM||||Just reading a majority of the posts which got me to thinking.<P>Wondering, if they kill the Aztek (hope! hope! hope! - fingers and toes crossed - buckin fugly) more then likely, somebody out there in the world will start an AZTEK Forum dedicated to the preservation of the Aztek (God forbid).   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> and double   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>===============<P>Now, regarding the gentleman with the 6 digit income comment...TOADSTOOL...errr I mean...Tryxalon   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>All of you are, of course, entitled to your opinion.<BR> <BR>However, none of you have admitted to 6 digit incomes so you cannot be THAT smart. <P>I also note that you all drive Fieros, ergo none of you are enamored with newer cars enough to give up your Fiero.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>FYI bought my house last November...paid for in full...no mortgage!<P>4,180 square foot house (not including three stahl attached (heated) garage and finished basement which would add another 1,500 sq feet to the aforementioned footage); under 4,000 sq foot unattached garage (c/w floor heat/AC and 14' high walls) all on 8.5 acres of property with full yard sprinkler system next to the Golf course (which brings up the value even more) with an additional 31 acres across the street with a nice barn and 8 horse stahls to boot.  <P>The shingles alone on my house cost more then most high paid people typically make in a year!  <P>V8Fiero and RossT know where I live and can vouch for my statements.<P>Picture(s) of the house to follow when I get my battery charger from Sony for my Digital and I can park all vehicles in my yard to show you!<P>Last March, ordered and purchased a new 2001 Dodge 3500 RAM extended cab with every option available.  Paid for in full...no financing what so ever!<P>All vehicles paid for in full ('87 Fiero, '97 Dodge 1500, 89 New Yorker, 87 LeBaron) and '97 Bobcat 743 Skid Steer Loader c/w Snowblower not to mention a '97 Dodge 3500 RAM extended cab I previously owned before ordering the 2001, which also was PIF.<P>And I still own my old 10 acres of property south of Rugby c/w house, barn, 2 quansets, 4 grain bins, shop, unattached garage and no neighbors.  And I never farmed a day in my life!<P>And I'm employed as a System Administrator/MIS Consultant for 15 years maintaining a computer network and coming up with better, more efficient and less expensive ways of doing the processes currently done with a computerized system...all mind you without a College/University degree (even though I attended college for 5 wasted years, first in Accounting then changed majors to Psychology, since they didn't have a University program for "Pimpology").<P>Its not what you make, what digit figures you pull down annualy and/or how smart you are.  <P>It is all about one's hobbies and passions.  Most of us here, the Fiero, no matter what it may cost us and/or whether we are rich and/or poor; smart as a whip and/or dumb as a box of rocks!  Boils down to choice.<P>So, hopefully I have blown the heck out of your quote regarding 6 digit incomes and/or having SMART's.<P>BTW, I was only 22 when I paid for my first house in FULL!  By myself and with no lienholders or any assistance what-so-ever.  Been through 2 houses since; farm and current...and I'm only 33 now!<P>How well are you doin!?!?!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||JSocha||06-11-2001||05:28 PM||||Oh...in addition to talking about our house that I forgot to mention.<P>All brick work on the outside of the house and attached as well as unattached garage; two tiers of brick layed herring bone patios c/w 8' retaining walls (in the highest area...all landscaping brick); all oak trim, doors, floors, stairs, cupboards; estimated 900 sq feet of glass windows and patio doors; ceramic floor tiles on all floors without the oak flooring; two fireplaces; two living rooms; built in oak bedroom cabinets and bed frames; 5 full bathrooms, 1/2 bath; 6 bedrooms; His & Hers walk in closets (wife's has built in safe); granite countertops; built in fridge; over two miles of heating tube for the heating/cooling system buried 24' feet in the ground (yes...that was said right); city water (even though we are rural) as we own the water line from town out to our place (over a mile!) through a special arrangement with the city (even out to the barn); cable access (again, even in the barn!) that noone else has due to the guy that built it put it all in himself (as he was a trenching contractor) and got the okay from the City!<P>In the process of turning the unattached garage into "The Socha Family, Bed & Breakfast" which will hopefully be two stories tall and just under 8,000 sq feet of living space setup as a house, which we hope to get started soon and completed by this fall or next spring.<P>So, if anyone is out there touring ND and coming through Rugby on Higway 2, let me know as you can stop in and either stay with us or visit a fellow Fiero owner and lover!||||reg||1
